1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,000
Lesson 1.2.

2
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:05,507
Statistical mechanics and
the Boltzmann distribution.

3
00:00:06,617 --> 00:00:11,991
If this course had a single formula, it
would be this: the probability of a state

4
00:00:11,991 --> 00:00:16,768
is proportional to the exponential
of minus its energy divided by k_B T.

5
00:00:17,148 --> 00:00:19,824
A thermodynamic chip
receives an energy function

6
00:00:19,824 --> 00:00:22,222
and returns samples
from that distribution.

7
00:00:22,602 --> 00:00:25,778
In this lesson we will see
where it comes from, what role

8
00:00:25,778 --> 00:00:29,178
temperature plays, and why the
number that normalizes it, the

9
00:00:29,178 --> 00:00:32,021
partition function, contains
all of thermodynamics.

10
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:36,499
Think of a small system in
contact with a huge bath.

11
00:00:36,879 --> 00:00:38,611
The total energy is fixed.

12
00:00:38,991 --> 00:00:42,340
Every unit of energy the
system holds is taken from the

13
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:45,750
bath, and the bath, by losing
energy, loses microstates.

14
00:00:46,130 --> 00:00:51,539
Since the bath's entropy grows linearly
with its energy, with slope one over T,

15
00:00:51,919 --> 00:00:56,074
its number of microstates
falls exponentially: every time

16
00:00:56,074 --> 00:01:00,302
the system takes a portion E
of energy, the number of ways

17
00:01:00,302 --> 00:01:03,947
to arrange the bath is
multiplied by e^(−E/k_B T).

18
00:01:04,327 --> 00:01:06,183
The bars show that count.

19
00:01:06,563 --> 00:01:09,575
The probability of a system
state is proportional to

20
00:01:09,575 --> 00:01:12,181
the number of microstates
it leaves the bath.

21
00:01:12,561 --> 00:01:14,336
That is the Boltzmann distribution.

22
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:19,583
Now take an energy landscape with
two valleys of different depth.

23
00:01:19,963 --> 00:01:22,606
The green curve is the
Boltzmann distribution.

24
00:01:22,986 --> 00:01:25,991
At high temperature, the
exponential is almost flat

25
00:01:25,991 --> 00:01:28,584
and the system explores
the whole landscape.

26
00:01:28,964 --> 00:01:32,024
As it cools, probability
concentrates in the valleys.

27
00:01:32,404 --> 00:01:35,677
And at very low temperature,
almost all the probability

28
00:01:35,677 --> 00:01:38,652
falls into the deepest
valley, the global minimum.

29
00:01:39,032 --> 00:01:42,266
Temperature is a knob between
exploring and optimizing.

30
00:01:42,646 --> 00:01:45,559
This same idea underlies
simulated annealing.

31
00:01:46,683 --> 00:01:50,306
For the probabilities
to sum to one we divide

32
00:01:50,306 --> 00:01:53,607
by Z, the sum of e^(−βE) over all states,

33
00:01:53,987 --> 00:01:57,299
with β equal to 1/k_B
T. Z looks like a mere

34
00:01:57,299 --> 00:02:00,986
normalizer, but it encodes
all of thermodynamics.

35
00:02:01,366 --> 00:02:06,139
The free energy is F = −k_B T ln Z.

36
00:02:06,519 --> 00:02:11,031
The mean energy is minus the derivative
of the log of Z with respect to β.

37
00:02:11,411 --> 00:02:14,330
And the variance of the energy
is the second derivative.

38
00:02:14,710 --> 00:02:17,849
The problem is that Z sums
over all states: for a

39
00:02:17,849 --> 00:02:20,987
hundred spins there are
two to the hundred terms.

40
00:02:21,367 --> 00:02:24,345
That is why, instead of
computing Z, we sample.

41
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:27,124
There is another elegant derivation.

42
00:02:27,504 --> 00:02:29,917
Let's look for the
distribution with maximum

43
00:02:29,917 --> 00:02:32,385
Shannon entropy that
has a fixed mean energy.

44
00:02:32,765 --> 00:02:37,560
With Lagrange multipliers, one for
normalization and one for energy, the

45
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:41,755
optimality condition says the
log of p is linear in the energy.

46
00:02:42,135 --> 00:02:47,660
That is, p is exponential in the
energy, and the multiplier is exactly β.

47
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:51,086
The Boltzmann distribution
is the least committed to

48
00:02:51,086 --> 00:02:53,957
everything we don't know,
except the mean energy.

49
00:02:55,083 --> 00:03:00,954
A three-level system with energies
zero, one and two, in units of k_B T.

50
00:03:01,334 --> 00:03:09,969
The Boltzmann weights are one, e^(−1)
and e^(−2): 1, 0.368 and 0.135.

51
00:03:10,349 --> 00:03:14,295
The partition function
is their sum, 1.503.

52
00:03:14,675 --> 00:03:20,893
The probabilities are
0.665, 0.245 and 0.09.

53
00:03:21,273 --> 00:03:26,377
The mean energy is 0.425,
and the free energy

54
00:03:26,377 --> 00:03:30,799
is minus the log of 1.503, about −0.41.

55
00:03:31,917 --> 00:03:36,213
This distribution is the programming
language of thermodynamic computing.

56
00:03:36,593 --> 00:03:40,265
A chip receives the parameters
of an energy and returns samples.

57
00:03:40,645 --> 00:03:46,670
In machine learning, energy-based
models learn that energy from data.

58
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:49,187
In optimization, lowering the temperature

59
00:03:49,187 --> 00:03:51,899
concentrates the distribution
on the global minimum.

60
00:03:52,279 --> 00:03:55,758
And with a quadratic energy,
the Boltzmann distribution

61
00:03:55,758 --> 00:03:58,794
is a Gaussian whose mean
solves a linear system.

62
00:03:59,917 --> 00:04:04,334
In the next lesson we will connect
this distribution with Gibbs entropy.

63
00:04:04,714 --> 00:04:09,239
In unit two we will see that it is the
stationary state of Langevin dynamics.

64
00:04:09,619 --> 00:04:13,235
And in unit four we will apply
it to the Ising model and to the

65
00:04:13,235 --> 00:04:16,623
pbit, which is the Boltzmann
distribution of a single spin.

66
00:04:17,750 --> 00:04:21,788
Careful: the formula gives the
probability of a microstate; for a

67
00:04:21,788 --> 00:04:25,391
macrostate you must multiply
by its number of microstates.

68
00:04:25,771 --> 00:04:29,471
Adding a constant to the energy
does not change the probabilities.

69
00:04:29,851 --> 00:04:32,797
Beta is one over k_B T, not one over T.

70
00:04:33,177 --> 00:04:36,162
And the distribution
describes equilibrium with a

71
00:04:36,162 --> 00:04:39,208
bath: out of equilibrium
we will need other tools.

72
00:04:40,317 --> 00:04:45,057
In summary: a system in contact
with a bath visits each state with

73
00:04:45,057 --> 00:04:49,079
probability e^(−βE)/Z, because
that maximizes the bath's

74
00:04:49,079 --> 00:04:53,533
microstates, or equivalently the
entropy at fixed mean energy.

75
00:04:53,913 --> 00:04:57,935
Temperature controls the shape of
the distribution, and the partition

76
00:04:57,935 --> 00:05:01,899
function contains the free energy
and all the moments of the energy.

77
00:05:03,017 --> 00:05:06,036
To practice: solve the two-level system.

78
00:05:06,416 --> 00:05:09,708
Prove the formulas for the
mean energy and its variance.

79
00:05:10,088 --> 00:05:12,392
Count the terms of the partition function

80
00:05:12,392 --> 00:05:14,641
for a hundred spins and for the Z1 chip.

81
00:05:15,021 --> 00:05:19,300
And apply Boltzmann to a spring to
obtain the equipartition theorem.
